**Verdict — partial.** Gemini’s corrected rank-three tube lemma and its averaged weighted Theorem J survive review. Two claims fail on exact examples: C2’s exponential orbit count in the stated ternary module, and G-L1’s primitive-point lookahead lemma. The 72-bit witness also distinguishes the analytic tube theorem from the measured pair-prefix cover.

### G-L2 — threshold covers and constructions

**Two-hyperplane lemma — correct with the corrected constant.** For \(v=L_i\times L_j\ne0\), the third row \(\bar v/\|v\|_2\) gives \(\det B=\|v\|_2\), including at complex places. For three independent box columns and tube widths \(s,u\), row Hadamard gives \(|\det(B\sigma A)|<9C_kE\,su\). Column Hadamard at the other \(e-1\) embeddings and \(|N\det A|\ge1\) then give
\[
\|v\|_2<3^{(3e+1)/2}(C_kE)^{3e-2}su.
\]
Thus \(u=2\tau/s\) requires the factor **2 in the denominator of \(\tau\)** used in the final report. A zero cross product makes the global product criterion vacuous. The dyadic count is repairable directly: rank-three Cramer bounds \(\log(M_i/t_i)=O_k(1+\log E)\), so \(O_k(1+\log E)\) scales per pair suffice. This is a two-form extension of the existing determinant obstruction. [Corrected proof](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L2g/details.txt:23) · [Cramer bound](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L2c/proof.txt:8)

**72-bit witness — measured pair-prefix coverage is correct; analytic coverage is false.** I checked all 240 same-base-embedding pairs and all four representatives of the unit orbit in the archived box. The smallest \(|L_iL_j|/(2\tau_{ij})\) was **36.245**. No continuous tube at any scale contains any representative. The global analytic cap was \(1.116\times10^{-59}\). The separately computed pair-prefix planes do contain the witness and explain all 15 smooth rank dimensions on this fixture. Rebuilding the single-form cover on the ternary box also captures its ternary representative. [Exact review checks](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L2c/review_checks.txt:1) · [Pair-prefix results](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L2g/results.json:45) · [Ternary plane](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L2c/ternary_witness.txt:1)

**C2 — parity rank correct; ternary orbit count wrong.** Write \(J=S^c\). Evaluating the claimed subset product at node \(r_j\) forces its Lagrange coordinate, for \(j\in J\), to be
\[
\frac{1}{\prod_{\ell\in J\setminus\{j\}}(r_j-r_\ell)}.
\]
If \(|J|\ge3\), the smallest integer node in \(J\) has a nonintegral coordinate. A base unit cannot clear it. Only singleton complements and adjacent-node pairs qualify, giving **at most \(2m-1\)** of the proposed products, seven of fourteen on the four test nodes. All formal subset products do have parity rank at most \(m\), by additivity of valuations. The 29-orbit, rank-four measurement used a **power-basis** box of radius two. It does not establish exponential growth in the Lagrange box or the necessity of both quotient clauses. [Interpolation proof](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L2c/review_certificates.txt:1) · [Basis switch in the experiment](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L2g/run_supplement.py:147)

**C1, C3, C4 — identities correct under their field hypotheses; general norm floors unsupported.** C1’s \(n-1\) small conjugates cannot bound all \(n\) coefficients: the reduced powers \(s^h\) stay bounded there while their coefficients become unbounded. A unimodular change of generators even makes a paired unit power an all-ones ternary candidate. C3’s repaired module is exactly a scalar multiple of the power module on each side; ideal normalization cancels those scalars. C4a’s Sperner count bounds the number of vanishing leading sums, but the remaining small conjugates still enter the norm. Its displayed cancelling vectors exceed the ternary box. C4b’s exponent follows for the specific fixed polynomial perturbation by taking its resultant in \(b\). The 74-bit supplement nevertheless fails the linking hypothesis: \(331\mid\Phi_{15}(600)\) and \((2/331)=-1\). These are construction diagnostics, with no general factoring exponent established. [Derivations](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L2c/review_certificates.txt:14) · [CM modulus check](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L2c/review_checks.txt:9)

### G-L1 — branch selection

**Theorem 1(a–c) — correct with the stated restrictions.** A good divisor makes \(v\) invertible, so it determines exactly one slope; the determinant bound gives uniqueness. The reverse search reduction needs \(Q_{\rm good}>2K^2\) and multiplies prime factors **one copy at a time**. The random-branch estimates follow from the binomial fiber bound and the truncated-divisor Rankin bound under the respective prime-band abundance assumptions in the full proof. The unrestricted estimate retains its \(K^{2\beta+o(1)}\) loss. The contribution is the exact M2 fiber count and its pointwise bounds. Neither bound prices target-dependent selection. [Theorem and proof](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L1g/full_proofs.md:20)

**Theorem 2 — correct as an averaged upper bound.** The new step is the multiplicity estimate for representations of a fixed \(z\) across the coefficient family. Its two terms have the same Rankin majorant, after which the earlier weighted Euler-product estimate cancels \(t_0\). The average varies the discriminant. Adaptive choice is covered within a portfolio fixed independently of the sampled form. The divisibility certificate works for any smooth modulus; exactly \(2^{\omega(d)}\) branches requires good split primes. [Multiplicity and cancellation](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L1g/full_proofs.md:59) · [Earlier weighted estimate](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/experiments/hatch/hatch_h1c/report.md:24)

**Auxiliary lemmas — mixed.** The independent-list collision bound needs sufficiently populous prime sets on both halves. Shortest-vector nonvisibility and same-root CRT scrambling are correct; neither proves an adaptive beam or walk loses its smoothness signal. **Tree-blindness is wrong.** Take \(Q=13u^2+4uv+45v^2\), \(N=581\), \(K=4\), \(B=5\). The good branch \(u=5v\bmod15\) extends modulus 3, yet its only nonzero symmetric-box points are \((0,\pm3)\). Minkowski supplies a lattice point without guaranteeing ambient primitivity. The claimed primitive density also misses \(\prod_{p\mid d}(1+1/p)^{-1}\). The proxy comparisons remain measurements, including the disclosed preregistered misses. [Counterexample](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L2c/review_certificates.txt:40) · [Claims and scorecard](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L1g/full_proofs.md:81)

### Codex scope checks

**C-L2 — correct under the stated hypotheses.** The conjugate determinant denominator and the replaced-column numerator give the claimed \((e/2)m\log m+O_k(m)\) loss. KH on \(V^h\) uses a unit-evaluation normalizer and the original ideals, so \(g_h=o(T)\) suffices. For \(\ell=o(m/\log x)\) deep tags, recording tags and intervals costs \(O_k(\ell\log x)=o(m)\) log choices. Each cell has relative envelope \(Z\le\log Y+Dm=o(x/T)\); KH forces noncoincident cell rank \(o(m)\). Large coincident graphs need the separate common-subfield bound. The new result is this direct restricted charge. [Threshold proof](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L2c/proof.txt:25) · [Cell proof](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L2c/proof.txt:72)

**C-L3 — correct refutation of unrestricted rank-to-probe inference.** Rank subtraction is rank–nullity. The coset table supplies genuine modular equalities with known factorizations and costs \(N^{1+o(1)}\), already charged as work. R separately assumes a quota of fresh successes. The natural accounting repair is \(r(V)\le r(U)+\#\mathrm{fresh}\), with \(U\) generated by other certificates, including modular equalities. Charging their first discovery restores rank accounting; a Dickman lower bound additionally requires control of \(r(U)\) within budget. [Table and rank proof](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L3c/lemma3.txt:14) · [R and credit rules](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/lowerbound_2_codex_merged.md:25)

**Accept as proved:** the corrected tube lemma, formal product parity bound, scoped G-L1 Theorems 1–2, conditional C-L2 bounds, and priced C-L3 counterexample.

**Single best next attack:** use the shared denominator in divided-difference modules of total pole degree \(O(m)\) to bound how many embeddings can simultaneously incur \(\log(M_i/t_i)\asymp m\log m\). C-L2 shows that threshold failure requires a positive fraction, and this family has the slow product growth needed to test that requirement. [Necessary condition](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L2c/proof.txt:42)